  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nAnother day of hot and mostly dry conditions and hazy/smoky skies \nreturn today with the Four Corners high still in place. The zonal \nflow pattern aloft will continue, keeping conditions dry for most of \nthe CWA today. Virga showers and dry thunderstorms will be possible \nacross southern portions, roughly along and south of a Cokeville-to-\nFarson-to-Muddy Gap line. Instability remains very weak, so these \nwill likely stay on the virga side just like what occurred \nyesterday. Wind gusts of 50 to 55 mph will be the main threat from \nthese \"Green Blobs\". The other concern today will be critical fire \nweather conditions. Red Flag Warnings are in effect today between \n18Z and 03Z Wednesday (12PM to 9PM) for all the same areas from \nyesterday except southern Lincoln County/Uinta County. RH values of \n8 to 12% will be widespread east of the Divide and include the \nJackson Valley and portions of Yellowstone. Overall, winds will not \nbe as strong across the CWA this afternoon but gusts of 25 to 30 mph \nwill occur in many of these areas. Needless to say, burning of any \nkind is highly discouraged today. Today will be the last day of this \nlatest heat wave with highs in the middle 90s for most areas east of \nthe Divide and upper 80s/lower 90s west of the Divide. \n\nThe pattern will begin to shift Wednesday, as the Four Corners high \nweakens and erodes and the flow aloft over the Cowboy State turns \nmore southwesterly irt an approaching trough. Precipitable water \nvalues will increase through the day as a result, with values \napproaching 1\". This won't be as strong as the surge of monsoonal \nmoisture that occurred a few weeks ago in July, but the threat for \nlocally heavy rain/localized flash flooding will certainly be in \nplace. Clouds will increase tonight into Wednesday morning leading \nto mostly cloudy skies across the forecast area. Temperatures will \nbe cooler as a result from increased cloud cover, about 3 to 8 \ndegrees cooler. Showers and thunderstorms will be widely scattered \nthrough the day, with western portions having the best chances \nthrough the afternoon. The HRRR brings an area of showers over the \nWind Corridor (Sweetwater to Natrona County), starting around \nsunrise and exiting early in the afternoon. Instability remains \nrather weak, likely due to the clouds, so not expecting any major \nthreats. Wind gusts of 40 to 50 mph and locally heavy rain will be \nthe most likely. \n\nThe trend of cooling temperatures continues into Thursday, with \ntemperatures dropping into the 70s to near 80 degrees. Thursday also \nlooks to be the best chance for widespread precipitation, especially \nacross western portions of the state, as the trough moves over ID. \nThe cooler and wetter conditions continue Friday, as the trough \nexits over the Northern Plains and the southwest flow pattern \ncontinues. This will be due to a strong high over the Deep \nSouth/Gulf Coast and a longwave trough over the West Coast. This \nwill allow embedded shortwaves to move over the Cowboy State, \ngenerating chances for convection across the area each day from \nFriday into the middle of next week. The threat for locally heavy \nrain will also continue as the monsoonal moisture stays in place as \na result of this pattern.",
